<?xml version="1.0"?>
<views>
  <view name="PRISM" rowsource="pwiz.Skyline.Model.Databinding.Entities.Transition" sublist="Results!*" uimode="proteomic">
    <column name="Precursor.Peptide.Protein" />
    <column name="Precursor.Peptide.Protein.Accession" />
    <column name="Precursor.Peptide.Protein.Gene" />
    <column name="Precursor.Peptide" />
    <column name="Precursor.Peptide.ModifiedSequence.UnimodIds" />
    <column name="Precursor.Charge" />
    <column name="Precursor.Mz" />
    <column name="Results!*.Value.PrecursorResult.IsotopeDotProduct" />
    <column name="Results!*.Value.PrecursorResult.DetectionQValue" />
    <column name="FragmentIon" />
    <column name="ProductCharge" />
    <column name="ProductMz" />
    <column name="Results!*.Value.Area" />
    <!--
      Background, so the MS2 signal accounting can total GROSS signal: Skyline's Area is
      background-subtracted (its own ChromPeakTest asserts that integrating without background gives
      Area + BackgroundArea) while an acquired total ion current is not, and dividing a net numerator
      by a gross denominator understates the assigned fraction. Cheap - it is already on ChromInfo.

      Skyline's ion-count columns are NOT here, and the reason is cost. They are the right measure -
      see qc_report.ms2_signal.measure - but Skyline computes them per spectrum for every
      transition. Measured on the 6.5 GB FLARE document (46M transition rows, 93 replicates), a
      three-column report exported in 9.5 minutes; the same report plus five LC Peak ion-count
      columns was 13% done after 63 minutes, writing at 4.5 MB/min against the baseline's 122 -
      27x slower per byte, projecting to 9-13 HOURS, single-threaded and holding 15 GB resident.
      Every PRISM run would pay that, for a QC section that is off by default. The per-transition
      column ALONE is 29x slower per row (7.9M rows in 42 minutes; about 4 hours for the document),
      so the cost is Skyline reading each transition's chromatogram points, not the column count.

      So the one column the accounting reads lives in Skyline-PRISM-Ions.skyr (view "PRISM-Ions"):
      this report plus LC Peak Transition Ion Count, exported instead of this one only when the user
      asks for ion counts. Keep the two column lists identical otherwise - PrismReportDefinitionTests
      checks - so a document merges the same whichever was exported.
    -->
    <column name="Results!*.Value.Background" />
    <column name="Results!*.Value.RetentionTime" />
    <column name="Results!*.Value.StartTime" />
    <column name="Results!*.Value.EndTime" />
    <column name="Results!*.Value.Fwhm" />
    <column name="Results!*.Value.ShapeCorrelation" />
    <column name="Results!*.Value.Truncated" />
    <column name="Results!*.Value.PrecursorResult.PeptideResult.ResultFile.Replicate.Name" />
    <column name="Results!*.Value.PrecursorResult.PeptideResult.ResultFile.FileName" />
    <column name="Results!*.Value.PrecursorResult.PeptideResult.ResultFile.TicArea" />
    <column name="Results!*.Value.PrecursorResult.PeptideResult.ResultFile.AcquiredTime" />
  </view>
</views>